  "download_disabled": false,
  "description": "<p dir=\"ltr\">This study used a multiple common-garden experiment with eight populations of Spartina alterniflora spanning its introduced range in China to examine phenotypic variation and covariance in key clonal traits. We found that low-latitude environments promoted earlier ramet emergence and greater ramet production, while low-latitude populations consistently exhibited a coordinated strategy of earlier emergence and higher ramet production. These findings highlight the potential role of clonal trait covariation in facilitating local adaptation of invasive plants across environmental gradients.</p>",
